  "summary": "Oakland's City Council is set to vote on a two-year, $2.25 million contract extension with Flock Safety for its network of nearly 300 ALPR cameras, after the proposal failed in the Public Safety Committee but was revived by the Rules Committee with less than 24 hours' notice. Critics including Councilmember Carroll Fife, the ACLU, and the Privacy Advisory Commission oppose the contract, citing reports that Oakland's Flock data was accessed by federal agencies including ICE and DHS, in apparent violation of state law and Oakland's sanctuary policy.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "I'm deeply concerned that this process feels like it lacks transparency and democracy. This item failed in committee and should go back to committee and be deliberated in that space.",
      "paragraph_idx": 3
    },
    {
      "quote": "more than 4,000 Oakland residents have urged the council to vote no on Flock expansion, and more than 40 organizations, including the ACLU of Northern California and multiple local unions, submitted a joint letter",
      "paragraph_idx": 5
    },
    {
      "quote": "city documents show that the data was made accessible and shared with at least six federal and a number of non-California state and local agencies, according to Hofer's lawsuit",
      "paragraph_idx": 12
    },
    {
      "quote": "I believe that we can use cameras for public safety tools, but this vendor has shown time and time again that they will just thwart the rule of law in cities and states all over the nation",
      "paragraph_idx": 17
    }
  ],
